Theorem IC-3: ParamContract componentwise max join + may_share OR

  Preconditions:
    - IC-1 has decomposed the CallGraph into SCCs in forward
      topological order (callees before callers) per
      Annex E §AIMS IC-1 + sec-7 PL-1a
    - IC-2 (proven separately) seeds every parameter with the
      most-optimistic OPTIMISTIC value
      `(Borrowed, Dead, Absent, BlockLocal, Unique,
      may_share=false)` per Annex E §AIMS IC-2
    - IC-3 type signature (per Interprocedural.proof lines 26-30):
        join_param_contract : ParamContract -> ParamContract ->
                              ParamContract
      whose output is the componentwise join of inputs, iterated
      across all observed call sites of the parameter's defining
      function within its SCC until no dimension changes
    - Spec statement (Annex E §AIMS IC-3): "Parameter
      contract join componentwise max: `access(max),
      consumption(max), cardinality(max), locality(max),
      uniqueness(max), may_share(OR)`. Iterate if any dim
      changes. `may_share` = per-parameter property (orthogonal
      to Locality); only `may_escape` was removed."
    - Shipped engine: `ParamContract::join` at
      compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/contract/mod.rs:390
      lines 390-412; called from
      ori_arc::aims::interprocedural::analyze_program SCC-fixpoint
      loop on every call-site demand-flow event
    - Per-dimension `join` operators are the lattice joins defined
      in Annex E §AIMS.1 (Access::join), sec-1.2
      (Consumption::join), sec-1.3 (Cardinality::join), sec-1.4
      (Uniqueness::join), sec-1.5 (Locality::join), each
      implemented as `std::cmp::max` over the chain order
    - `may_share` is a `bool` joined by short-circuit OR (`||`)
      per Annex E §AIMS IC-3 "may_share(OR)"
    - Target-vs-shipped axis carve-out (per Annex E §AIMS
      "Target-only / partially-shipped subsystems" top-of-file):
      shipped ParamContract::join also folds three IC-4 return-flow
      fields (`transfers_through_return: ||`, `return_alias:
      ReturnAliasShape::join`, `return_payload_contains_param: ||`)
      and the target-vs-shipped legacy `may_escape: ||`. IC-3
      proof discharges the 5 spec lattice dims + may_share; the
      auxiliary fields ride alongside under the same monotone
      OR / max-join discipline (verified in §IC-4)
    - L-1, L-2, L-3, L-4, L-6 lemmas from sec-1.8 are proven in
      §02 of this plan (Plan-Bug DAG) and provide commutativity,
      associativity, idempotence, partial-order axiom, and
      monotonicity of the per-dimension joins

  Soundness property:
    Forall ParamContract instances p, q.
      Let r := join_param_contract(p, q) be the emitted result.
      Six conjuncts SHALL hold per spec Annex E §AIMS IC-3:

      (P1) Access componentwise max:
        r.access = max(p.access, q.access)
        under the chain order `Borrowed < Owned` per sec-1.1.

      (P2) Consumption componentwise max:
        r.consumption = max(p.consumption, q.consumption)
        under the chain order `Dead < Linear < Affine <
        Unrestricted` per sec-1.2.

      (P3) Cardinality componentwise max:
        r.cardinality = max(p.cardinality, q.cardinality)
        under the chain order `Absent < Once < Many` per
        sec-1.3.

      (P4) Locality componentwise max:
        r.locality_bound = max(p.locality_bound,
                               q.locality_bound)
        under the chain order `BlockLocal < FunctionLocal <
        ArgEscaping < HeapEscaping < Unknown` per sec-1.5.

      (P5) Uniqueness componentwise max:
        r.uniqueness = max(p.uniqueness, q.uniqueness)
        under the chain order `Unique < MaybeShared < Shared`
        per sec-1.4.

      (P6) `may_share` boolean OR:
        r.may_share = p.may_share || q.may_share
        per sec-5 IC-3 may_share(OR) clause.

      Plus three lattice-property conjuncts inherited from
      sec-1.8 + standard product-lattice algebra:

      (P7) Commutativity:
        join_param_contract(p, q) = join_param_contract(q, p)
        per L-1: each per-dim `max` is commutative; `||` is
        commutative; componentwise composition preserves
        commutativity.

      (P8) Associativity:
        join_param_contract(join_param_contract(p, q), r)
          = join_param_contract(p, join_param_contract(q, r))
        per L-2: each per-dim `max` is associative; `||` is
        associative; componentwise composition preserves
        associativity.

      (P9) Monotonicity (per parameter, per dim):
        p ≤ q ⟹ join_param_contract(p, q) = q
        and for any r, join_param_contract(p, r) ≤
        join_param_contract(q, r)
        per L-6: monotonicity of each per-dim join lifts to the
        product.

  Proof obligation:
    Nine-part discharge corresponding to (P1)..(P9); per-dim
    conjuncts (P1)..(P6) ground in the shipped Rust line-by-line
    structural match to the spec; lattice-property conjuncts
    (P7)..(P9) ground in lemmas L-1, L-2, L-6 from sec-1.8.

    Per-dimension parts (P1)..(P5) — uniform structural match:

      The shipped `ParamContract::join` at mod.rs lines 390-412
      reads (collapsed to the 5 spec lattice dimensions; the 3
      IC-4 return-flow fields + 1 target-vs-shipped may_escape
      field ride alongside per the Target-vs-shipped axis carve-
      out and are verified in their own theorems):

        pub fn join(&self, other: &Self) -> Self {
            Self {
                access: self.access.join(other.access),
                consumption: self.consumption.join(other.consumption),
                cardinality: self.cardinality.join(other.cardinality),
                locality_bound: self.locality_bound.join(other.locality_bound),
                uniqueness: self.uniqueness.join(other.uniqueness),
                may_share: self.may_share || other.may_share,
                ...
            }
        }

      Each per-dim `Class::join` is implemented as
      `std::cmp::max` over the canonical chain (verified in
      §02 / lattice properties round); `||` is short-circuit
      boolean OR.

      Part (P1) — `access.join` IS `max` over `Borrowed < Owned`
      by sec-1.1; line 392 structurally matches spec.

      Part (P2) — `consumption.join` IS `max` over `Dead <
      Linear < Affine < Unrestricted` by sec-1.2; line 393
      structurally matches spec.

      Part (P3) — `cardinality.join` IS `max` over `Absent <
      Once < Many` by sec-1.3; line 394 structurally matches
      spec.

      Part (P4) — `locality_bound.join` IS `max` over
      `BlockLocal < FunctionLocal < ArgEscaping < HeapEscaping
      < Unknown` by sec-1.5; line 397 structurally matches
      spec. The shipped struct preserves `locality_bound` as
      the SSOT dimension name; spec Annex E §AIMS.5
      "Both `seq_add` and `alt_join` coincide with `join` for
      Locality (escape widens monotonically)" — IC-3's join
      is `alt_join` (CFG-merge / call-site-merge) which equals
      lattice join for Locality.

      Part (P5) — `uniqueness.join` IS `max` over `Unique <
      MaybeShared < Shared` by sec-1.4; line 398 structurally
      matches spec.

      Coherence side-conditions for (P1)..(P5):

        - CN-8 (sec-2): `Access = Borrowed ∧ Locality >
          FunctionLocal ⟹ Locality := FunctionLocal`.
          Application after join: if max of two `access`
          chains yields Borrowed AND max of two `locality`
          chains yields ArgEscaping/HeapEscaping/Unknown,
          CN-8 clamps locality back to FunctionLocal in a
          post-join canonicalization round. Per Annex E §AIMS.8 L-8 "Canonicalization SHALL preserve joins:
          `canonicalize(a ⊔ b) = a ⊔ b`" — the clamp does
          NOT change the join's semantic identity; it only
          maps the result into the canonical-state subset
          (cross-dim feasibility per sec-2 opener). IC-3
          remains the join operator regardless of CN-8's
          post-processing.

        - CN-6 (sec-2): `Locality ≥ HeapEscaping ∧ Uniqueness =
          Unique ⟹ Uniqueness := MaybeShared`. Similar
          analysis — canonicalization preserves the join under
          L-8.

        - CN-1 (sec-2): `Consumption = Dead ⟺ Cardinality =
          Absent` bidirectional. The component joins respect
          this: `max(Dead, X) = X` and `max(Absent, X) = X`
          (any non-bottom value in one component "drags" the
          other component up, then CN-1 enforces the bidir
          equivalence). Verified at the canonicalization
          round following join (also proven in §03
          canonicalization).

    Part (P6) — `may_share` boolean OR:

      `may_share` is `bool` joined by `||` per sec-5 IC-3.
      Lattice structure of `bool` under OR: `false < true`,
      height 1, join = `||`, bottom = `false`, top = `true`.
      Line 396 of mod.rs reads:
        `may_share: self.may_share || other.may_share`
      which is the canonical lattice-join for the 2-element
      boolean chain. Structural match to spec.

      Coherence with sec-1.5 note "`may_share` = per-parameter
      property (orthogonal to Locality)": no canonicalization
      rule (CN-1 through CN-8) mutates `may_share`, so the
      OR-join survives the post-join canonicalization round
      unchanged.

      Operationally: `may_share = true` after IC-3 join means
      AT LEAST ONE observed call site had `may_share = true`
      (callee RcInc's the param, OR a downstream callee in
      the SCC does). DP-9 (`cow_mode`) consumes `may_share`
      via Annex E §AIMS DP-9 third row:
      `MaybeShared ∧ param with IC-3 ParamContract.uniqueness
      = Unique ⟹ StaticUnique`. The DP-9 fast path requires
      the converged contract's Uniqueness, not `may_share` —
      `may_share` flows into the EffectSummary OR-join
      (sec-5 IC-5) and RL-30 LLVM attribute emission (sec-8).

    Part (P7) — Commutativity discharge:

      Goal: `r := join(p, q) = join(q, p)`.

      Per-dim:
        max(p.access, q.access) = max(q.access, p.access)
          per L-1 (`max` is commutative on totally-ordered
          chains; Birkhoff 1967, Lattice Theory §I.5).
        Same for consumption, cardinality, locality_bound,
        uniqueness — all use `max` on totally-ordered chains.

        p.may_share || q.may_share = q.may_share || p.may_share
          per algebra of boolean OR (commutative monoid identity:
          OR is commutative; standard result).

      Conjunction of six commutative per-dim operators ⟹
      `join_param_contract` is commutative on the product. (P7)
      discharged.

    Part (P8) — Associativity discharge:

      Goal: `join(join(p, q), r) = join(p, join(q, r))`.

      Per-dim:
        max(max(p.access, q.access), r.access)
          = max(p.access, max(q.access, r.access))
          per L-2 (`max` is associative on totally-ordered
          chains; Birkhoff 1967, §I.5).
        Same for consumption, cardinality, locality_bound,
        uniqueness — all use `max` on totally-ordered chains.

        (p.may_share || q.may_share) || r.may_share
          = p.may_share || (q.may_share || r.may_share)
          per algebra of boolean OR (associative monoid).

      Conjunction of six associative per-dim operators ⟹
      `join_param_contract` is associative on the product. (P8)
      discharged.

      Consequence: N-ary join `join(p_1, p_2, ..., p_n)` is
      well-defined and fold-order-independent per sec-6 IA-9
      "N-ary join at CFG merges SHALL be permutation-invariant:
      fold order over successor states SHALL NOT affect the
      result (follows from L-1 + L-2)". (P7) + (P8) together
      discharge sec-6 IA-9 for the ParamContract product.

    Part (P9) — Monotonicity discharge:

      Goal: `p ≤ q ⟹ join(p, q) = q`, and for any r,
      `join(p, r) ≤ join(q, r)`.

      First clause: per L-4 partial-order axiom "`a ≤ b ⟺
      a ⊔ b = b`". Apply componentwise:
        p ≤ q (componentwise) ⟺ for each dim d,
                                p.d ≤ q.d in the dim's chain
        ⟺ for each dim d, max(p.d, q.d) = q.d
        ⟺ join(p, q) = q componentwise = q
      First clause discharged.

      Second clause (monotonicity of join over the partial
      order): per L-6 "Transfer functions SHALL be monotone:
      `a ≤ b ⟹ f(a) ≤ f(b)`". The function
      `λr. join_param_contract(r, fixed_other)` is monotone in
      `r`:
        p ≤ q ⟹ for each dim d, p.d ≤ q.d
              ⟹ for each dim d, max(p.d, fixed_other.d) ≤
                                max(q.d, fixed_other.d)
                (monotonicity of `max` on totally-ordered
                chains)
              ⟹ join(p, fixed_other) ≤ join(q, fixed_other)
                componentwise
      Same argument for `may_share` (OR is monotone in each
      argument over `false < true`). Second clause discharged.

      Consequence: IC-3 join, applied iteratively in the SCC
      fixpoint loop, monotonically advances every
      ParamContract toward conservative. Combined with IC-2's
      bottom seed (Tarski 1955 least-fixpoint theorem, cited
      in IC-2 §S3) the converged contract is the least
      conservative bound consistent with the SCC's observed
      call-site demands. (P9) discharged.

    Convergence side-condition (cross-cite to IC-7):

      Iteration terminates because:
        (a) Each per-dim chain has finite height per sec-1.8 L-5
            (Access 1, Consumption 3, Cardinality 2, Locality
            4, Uniqueness 2; sum 12).
        (b) `may_share` has height 1 (false → true).
        (c) Per-iteration, at least one dim of at least one
            param's ParamContract strictly advances (otherwise
            the iteration ends).
        (d) Sum of all per-param height advances is bounded by
            `param_count × 13` (= 12 lattice dims + may_share
            boolean), plus the IC-4 return-flow + EffectSummary
            + ContextBehavior + may_escape advances.

      IC-7 (proven separately) gives the closed-form iteration
      bound `param_count × 13 + 8 + 6 + 4` per Annex E §AIMS IC-7 (spec formula) or `param_count × 17 + 8 + 5 + 4`
      per the shipped formula at
      compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/interprocedural/
      mod.rs lines 248-267 (Target-vs-shipped axis carve-out:
      shipped formula absorbs the may_escape legacy field +
      the 3 IC-4 return-flow fields into `× 17`, then sums
      param_height + return_height + effect_height +
      context_height; shipped and spec formulas are byte-
      identical at 17p + 17). Convergence is therefore
      guaranteed.

    Coverage gate — corpus enumeration:

