    Negative-direction witness — non-monotone join rejected:

      Take Row J3 (per_dim_advance_access). Suppose hypothetical
      implementer replaces `max` with `min` on the Access dim:
        join((Borrowed, ...), (Owned, ...)) →
          (min(Borrowed, Owned), ...) = (Borrowed, ...)

      Per (P9) monotonicity: the corrupted join is anti-monotone
      — joining with a strictly larger ParamContract makes the
      result SMALLER. Combined with IC-2's bottom seed, the
      Tarski-iterate sequence DESCENDS instead of ASCENDING —
      it cannot converge to the least upper bound of observed
      demands. Worse, the iteration may cycle: if one call site
      passes Owned and another passes Borrowed, alternate
      iterations flip the contract's Access between Owned and
      Borrowed indefinitely (no monotone progress, no fixpoint
      reached).

      The corrupted contract poisons DP-1 (`is_rc_needed`):
      `access = Owned ∧ consumption ≠ Dead` becomes false on
      iterations where the corrupted join settled at Borrowed,
      causing missing RcInc emission at call sites where the
      callee will RcDec. Result: double-free or use-after-free
      at runtime — exactly the AIMS Invariant 1 ("Contracts and
      realization must agree" per Annex E §AIMS §2) violation.

      L-6 monotonicity is therefore load-bearing for the
      entire interprocedural RC discipline; (P9) is not a
      stylistic property but a soundness foundation.

    Negative-direction witness — non-commutative join rejected:

      Suppose hypothetical implementer makes Access::join
      non-commutative (e.g., always returns `self.access`,
      ignoring `other.access`). Per L-1 commutativity failure:
      iteration order over the SCC's call-site demand-flow
      events changes the converged contract. Two functionally
      equivalent compilation runs (same program, different
      compiler-internal call-graph traversal order) produce
      DIFFERENT ParamContracts — the contract is no longer a
      function of the program. The deterministic query contract
      ("deterministic — no random/time/IO") is violated; the
      memoization correctness contract is broken.

      Commutativity (P7) is therefore load-bearing for
      compilation determinism; non-commutative joins are
      banned at the lattice level (sec-1.8 L-1).

    Negative-direction witness — non-associative N-ary join
    rejected:

      Suppose hypothetical implementer makes Consumption::join
      non-associative. Per L-2 associativity failure: N-ary
      fold over a list of call-site demands produces different
      results depending on fold direction (left-fold vs
      right-fold vs balanced reduce). sec-6 IA-9
      "permutation-invariant" SHALL hold; N-ary fold direction
      is a compiler-internal implementation detail (worklist
      ordering); making the converged contract depend on it
      destroys determinism in the same way as (P7) failure.

      Associativity (P8) is therefore load-bearing for N-ary
      fold correctness across compiler-internal traversal
      orders (sec-6 IA-9).

    IC-3 dependencies — leaf within AIMS contract layer:

      IC-3 consumes per-dim `Class::join` operators (proven in
      §02 lattice properties) and produces componentwise-joined
      ParamContract values. It reads no AimsStateMap, no
      per-variable lattice state, and depends on IC-2's
      OPTIMISTIC seed only at iteration boundaries (each
      iteration consumes the previous-iteration's contract,
      not IC-2 directly after iteration 1). The dependency
      chain at the proof-obligation level is acyclic:
        IC-1 --> IC-2 (parameter contract seeding)
        IC-2 --> IC-3 (parameter contract componentwise join)
        L-1/L-2/L-6 (§02 lattice properties) --> IC-3
        IC-3 --> IC-4 (return contract componentwise join,
                         uses the same join shape)
        IC-3 --> IC-5 (effect summary OR-join + per-instr
                         derivation)
        IC-3 --> IC-7 (convergence bound — consumes
                         per-param chain height to compute
                         iteration bound)

      IC-3 is the join-operator leaf within the IC layer; L-*
      lemmas are proven upstream in §02.

    Algorithm-citation declaration:
      algorithm: componentwise product-lattice join
                           over a six-tuple of finite chains +
                           OR-join over boolean
      published basis: Birkhoff, G., "Lattice Theory",
                           3rd ed., American Mathematical
                           Society Colloquium Publications,
                           vol. XXV, 1967, §I.5 (totally-
                           ordered chains under max), §V
                           (product lattices); Cousot & Cousot,
                           "Abstract Interpretation: A Unified
                           Lattice Model for Static Analysis
                           of Programs by Construction or
                           Approximation of Fixpoints",
                           POPL 1977 (interprocedural
                           summary computation via SCC-
                           topological monotone iteration with
                           componentwise lattice joins)
      complexity: O(1) per `join` invocation (six
                           constant-time `max` operations + one
                           boolean OR + three Option/bool
                           target-vs-shipped axis carve-out
                           field operations); O(param_count ×
                           call_site_count × height_sum) total
                           per SCC convergence per IC-7's
                           bound
      determinism: deterministic; `std::cmp::max` and
                           `||` are pure deterministic
                           operations; commutativity (P7) +
                           associativity (P8) ensure fold-order
                           independence per sec-6 IA-9
      reference impl: `ParamContract::join` at
                           compiler/ori_arc/src/
                           aims/contract/mod.rs lines 390-412;
                           invoked from
                           ori_arc::aims::interprocedural::
                           analyze_program SCC-fixpoint loop
      cross-language Lean 4 src/Lean/Compiler/IR/Borrow.lean
      prior art: uses identical componentwise max-
                           join over borrow-inference parameter
                           tuples; Cousot/Cousot abstract-
                           interpretation framework is the
                           canonical theoretical foundation
                           for interprocedural fixpoint
                           analysis with componentwise lattice
                           joins; arc.md §Reference Repos
                           cites Lean 4 + Swift ARC + Koka
                           Perceus as the prior art set

